$A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      3 & 6 \\
      {- 1} & {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $B = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      4 & 3 \\
      {- 1} & {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$

Verify that $\text{det \,\,}A = \text{det \,\,}B + \text{det \,\,}C$, where $$A~ = ~\begin{bmatrix}
      {a + e} & {b + f} \\
      c & d
      \end{bmatrix},~~~B~ = ~\begin{bmatrix}
      a & b \\
      c & d
      \end{bmatrix},~~C = \begin{bmatrix}
      e & f \\
      c & d
      \end{bmatrix}$$

[T] If $\text{det \,\,}A$ is close to zero, is the matrix $A$ nearly singular? Experiment with the nearly singular $4 \times 4$ matrix $$A = \left\lbrack \begin{matrix}
      {~~4} & {~0} & {- 7} & {- 7} \\
      {- 6} & {~~1} & {~~11} & {~~~9} \\
      {~~~7} & {- 5} & {~~10} & {~19} \\
      {- 1} & {~~~2} & {~~~~3} & {- 1}
      \end{matrix}~ \right\rbrack$$
